TinyDict

Description
A minimalistic, high-performance, and ultra-compact key-value hash table written in pure C99 for resource-constrained systems. It operates on a keyless append-only principle: original keys are immediately converted into internal 128-bit hashes ('__uint128_t') and discarded, while individual entry deletion is prohibited to prevent memory fragmentation.

Advantages
Minimal Memory Footprint: Indexing overhead is reduced to an absolute minimum by completely eliminating the storage of original source keys.
Cache Locality: Data is stored in a flat array of opaque elements, optimizing CPU cache utilization and speeding up data retrieval.
Stack Allocation Support: The control block size is exported via the 'DICTIONARY_SIZE' constant, allowing users to instantiate the dictionary on the stack without using 'malloc'.
High API Reliability: A Fail-Fast design with separate channels for error codes and payload data (as seen in 'TryGetValue') completely eliminates the ambiguity of 'NULL' returns.
Autonomy and Safety: The library avoids global state, strictly forbids calling 'exit()' (using 'return' instead), and ensures diagnostic logs are immediately visible by forcing 'fflush(stderr)'.
Binary Persistence: Built-in Serialization() dumps the flat elements array directly to disk—automatically generating a timestamped file via the 'DDMMYYhhmmss.bin' mask if no path is provided—while Deserialization() reads the binary layout to safely rebuild the control fields and heap-allocated boundaries from scratch.

Disadvantages
Probabilistic Nature: Because original keys are discarded, structural collision verification is impossible; operational correctness relies entirely on the uniqueness of the 128-bit hash.
Restricted Immutability: Individual entry deletion is not supported; the data structure can only be reset atomically via 'ClearDictionary'.
Mixed Heap Allocation Pattern: Despite being optimized for embedded environments, most retrieval functions ('TryGetValue', 'GetCopyValue', etc.) enforce internal heap allocations.
Risk of Undefined Behavior: Demands manual data alignment control from the user when allocating the dictionary control block on the stack.

Build
Requires a C99-compliant compiler and CMake 4.1.2+.

Using CMake
cmake -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DCMAKE_C_FLAGS="-march=native -flto" .
cmake --build build --parallel

Without CMake (Direct GCC Compilation)

Static Library ('.a')
gcc -std=c99 -pedantic-errors -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -O3 -march=native -flto -c dict.c hash.c exception.c
ar rcs libtinydict.a dict.o hash.o exception.o

Dynamic Library ('.dll' / '.so' / '.dylib')

Windows (MinGW):
  gcc -std=c99 -pedantic-errors -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -O3 -march=native -flto -shared dict.c hash.c exception.c -o libtinydict.dll -Wl,--out-implib,libtinydict.dll.a

Linux / macOS: (For macOS, replace '.so' with '.dylib')
  gcc -std=c99 -pedantic-errors -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -O3 -march=native -flto -fPIC -c dict.c hash.c exception.c
  gcc -shared -flto dict.o hash.o exception.o -o libtinydict.so

Test
If you want to test the library, use the following compilation command:
gcc -std=c99 -O3 -march=native -flto test.c -L. -ltinydict -o dict_prog
